Certainly! Here's a simple yet effective custom client-side router in vanilla JavaScript for a single-page application (SPA). This router leverages modern JavaScript features such as `history.pushState`, `popstate` event, and dynamic content rendering without any external frameworks.

---

### Basic Concept
- Use the URL hash (`#`) or the History API (`pushState`) to manage routes.
- Listen for navigation events (`click` on links, `popstate` event).
- Render content dynamically based on the current route.

---

### Example Implementation

```javascript
// Define your routes and their corresponding render functions
const routes = {
  '/': () => `<h1>Home Page</h1><p>Welcome to the homepage!</p>`,
  '/about': () => `<h1>About Page</h1><p>Learn more about us.</p>`,
  '/contact': () => `<h1>Contact Page</h1><p>Contact us at contact@example.com.</p>`,
  // Add more routes as needed
};

// Select the container where content will be rendered
const appContainer = document.getElementById('app');

// Function to handle route changes
function handleRoute() {
  const path = window.location.pathname;
  const routeRender = routes[path] || (() => `<h1>404 Not Found</h1><p>The page does not exist.</p>`);
  appContainer.innerHTML = routeRender();
}

// Function to navigate programmatically
function navigateTo(path) {
  history.pushState({}, '', path);
  handleRoute();
}

// Set up event listeners for link clicks
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const target = e.target;
  if (target.tagName === 'A' && target.hasAttribute('data-link')) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const path = target.getAttribute('href');
    navigateTo(path);
  }
});

// Handle browser navigation (back/forward)
window.addEventListener('popstate', handleRoute);

// Initialize the router
window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  handleRoute();
});
```

---

### Usage in HTML

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Vanilla JS SPA Router</title>
</head>
<body>
  <nav>
    <a href="/" data-link>Home</a>
    <a href="/about" data-link>About</a>
    <a href="/contact" data-link>Contact</a>
  </nav>
  <div id="app"></div>

  <script src="router.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### How it works:
- Clicking on links with `data-link` prevents default navigation and uses `pushState` to change the URL without reloading.
- The `handleRoute` function updates the content based on the current path.
- The `popstate` event ensures back/forward buttons work correctly.
- You can extend the `routes` object with more paths and render functions.

---

### Notes:
- For more advanced routing, consider adding route parameters, query string parsing, or nested routes.
- This example uses `history.pushState` for cleaner URLs; if you prefer hash-based routing, replace `window.location.pathname` with `window.location.hash` and adjust accordingly.

---

